+# Optional local Laya shadow advice
+
+The Python reviewer can request bounded specialist-topic hints from the existing local Laya service after mechanically validating a candidate. It emits `system_one_hint` progress metadata. It does not edit the claim, attack class, verifier prompt, verdict, severity, suppression policy or review budget. No raw source excerpts, prompts or classifier response are logged.
+
+Set `REVIEWER_LAYA_URL` and `REVIEWER_LAYA_API_KEY` in the trusted reviewer process to enable it. Default checkpoint: `typed-decisions`; optional `REVIEWER_LAYA_MODEL`. `REVIEWER_LAYA_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` defaults to 2, with a maximum of 5. Only eight shadow calls per verification stage; unavailable or invalid advice stops the branch. Leave the variables unset to disable it. Keys must not enter agent prompts or tool environments. Use a reachable host/LAN URL for container deployments; container localhost is not the Windows GPU host.
+
+The client removes common credential forms and sends only bounded candidate metadata, never evidence quotes. Approve the metadata projection for the deployment: heuristic redaction does not prove absence of every confidential value. Existing independent verification stays authoritative. Warm the explicit checkpoint before short-timeout requests; process initialization can exceed the client timeout.
+
+Local evaluation matched seven of seven topic references with typed-decisions, all below the illustrative 0.8 confidence threshold. This small corpus does not validate automatic specialist reassignment or vulnerability verification. Scanner labels and deterministic priorities take precedence.
+
+Full guide: http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs/system-one-integrations.md while the host service is running. Frozen results live in the sibling Laya project, `docs/benchmark/REPORT.md`. Production deployment remains separate from this local change.
+
+Source-skill manifests hash bytes. The scoped `.gitattributes` rule preserves LF under `docs/assets/skills`; Windows CRLF conversion otherwise breaks the existing manifest check. The original expected hashes remain intact.
